{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The finding\n\nEpic 6202's story **S1 (`aa40-cbda`) is CLOSED, but its deliverable is not in effect.** The\ngate-scratch volume exists, is attached, and is raw and unmounted; gate scratch runs on the ROOT\nfilesystem -- precisely the silent fallback S1 exists to prevent.\n\n## Measured on the live host (SSM, read-only)\n\n```\nlsblk    nvme2n1  50G  no FSTYPE, no MOUNTPOINT   serial vol06780b8557d1416b7\nblkid /dev/nvme2n1        -> no filesystem at all\n/etc/fstab                -> /, /boot/efi, /var/gerrit ONLY; no gate-scratch line\nmountpoint gate-scratch   -> is not a mountpoint\n.gate-scratch-required    -> ABSENT      .gate-scratch-mounted -> ABSENT\ndf -h /                   -> 49G of 60G (81%) as of 2026-09-05 22:24 UTC\n```\n\nAWS confirms the volume is `in-use`, attached to `i-00880b2c7f13527c5` at `/dev/sdg`, with a\nmatching NVMe serial -- so there is no ambiguity about which device it is. It simply was never\nformatted or mounted. **Root has grown from 57% to 81%**; see the re-verification comment for the\ncurrent reading and two findings that change the remediation sequence.\n\n## Why it happened\n\nThe volume was created by a `terraform apply` AFTER the instance's first boot. `user_data.sh`\nruns under cloud-init on FIRST BOOT ONLY, so its `mount_ebs_volume \"${gate_scratch_volume_id}\"`\nstep has never executed for this volume, and nothing else on the host mounts it. The script is\ncorrect; it simply never ran. That is a gap in the delivery path, not a logic bug.\n\n## The monitoring worked -- and is worth crediting\n\nBoth alarms are correctly in ALARM. `rebar-gate-scratch-unmounted` reads `gate_scratch_mounted`\n= 0. `rebar-gate-scratch-disk-high` publishes NOTHING (no mount to `df`) and, because\n`treat_missing_data = \"breaching\"`, alarms rather than reading healthy -- the dead-man-correct\nbehaviour `bff5-9163` was closed for. So this WAS detectable and detected. What was missing is\nthat S1 closed on code correctness without a check that the volume entered service.\n\n## Why this is DEGRADED, not BROKEN, and why remediation is not a simple mount\n\n`user_data.sh:236-238` writes two markers to deliberately different filesystems (`chmod 0700` at\n`:236`, declaration `:237`, proof `:238`; contract in the comment at `:222-228`). The range this\nticket first cited, `:123-130`, is the device-resolution `readlink -f` branch from bug `ad8d` F5\nand has nothing to do with markers. `.gate-scratch-required` sits beside the mount point on ROOT\n(surviving an unmount); `.gate-scratch-mounted` sits inside it on the VOLUME. Declaration present\n+ proof absent makes gate admission REFUSE rather than repopulate on root.\n\nHere BOTH are absent, so admission reads \"this host has no dedicated volume\" and gates run on\nroot. Stable, hence not p1 -- but the isolation S1 bought is not being had, and a gate run that\nfills root now takes Gerrit down with it.\n\n`/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch` holds LIVE, in-use working directories. Mounting over them HIDES\nthem from any process that opens them afterwards while stranding the bytes on root, and doing it\nmid-review breaks that run. The contents are genuinely scratch, so nothing needs migrating -- but\na quiet window does, and the stranded copy must be removed or the space is lost twice. The full\nordered procedure -- quiesce, MOVE the contents aside (never mount over them), format,\nfstab-by-UUID, mount, assert `mountpoint -q` BEFORE any marker, write the markers proof-first,\nRECREATE the review-bot container, reclaim, then confirm from CloudWatch -- lives in\n`infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md`, with a verification and a rollback per step. Do not\nimprovise from this summary.\n\n## Scope split: what lands in code, and what only an operator can do\n\nAC1-AC4 and AC6 are HOST actions on a running production box. No agent performs them, so they\nare tagged `[non-codebase]` and are discharged by an operator executing\n`infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md` and recording the result here. What lands as code is that\nrunbook plus AC5/AC7's delivery-gap assertion. That is not a descope: for AC1-AC4 the risky part\nis the SEQUENCE, so a turnkey, verified, rollback-per-step procedure IS the deliverable.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The gate-scratch volume is formatted, in `/etc/fstab` by UUID, and mounted at\n      `/var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch`. Proven by `mountpoint -q` and `lsblk` showing the fstype.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] Both markers exist on their intended filesystems, created in the safe order, and\n      `chmod 0700` is applied. Proven by `ls` plus confirming they are on different devices.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] `rebar/host:gate_scratch_mounted` publishes 1 and `disk_used_percent` resumes publishing;\n      both alarms return to OK. Proven from CloudWatch, not from the host alone.\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The stranded copy under root is reclaimed and `df /` reflects it.\n- [ ] AC5 (the only NON-`[non-codebase]` criterion). Concrete edit target:\n      **`scripts/assert_volumes_in_service.py`** (new), with its offline oracle\n      `tests/unit/test_assert_volumes_in_service_dcc3.py` (new) and an invocation section in\n      `infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md`. It joins the two halves nobody had joined —\n      terraform's `aws_volume_attachment` set (which volumes are attached) against the host's\n      mount table (which mount points are backed by a real filesystem) — and fails, naming the\n      volume, when a volume is attached but not in service. Three outcomes, not two: `UNKNOWN`\n      (the host did not report the device) is distinct from `NOT-IN-SERVICE` and also fails.\n      See AC7 for why a reconciler is not the chosen branch. Flipping\n      `rebar.llm.gate_admission` to refuse on a missing declaration would refuse every gate on\n      this box while it is unmounted; filed as `reportable-commutual-rooster`\n      (`1064-8c4a-f399-41de`).\n- [ ] [non-codebase] The review-bot container is RECREATED after the host mount, and the mount is\n      verified FROM INSIDE it. `infra/compose/docker-compose.yml:305` binds `/var/lib/rebar` —\n      the PARENT — and Docker binds default to `rprivate` propagation, so a mount created on the\n      host underneath a running container does NOT appear inside it. Without this the bot keeps\n      writing to the shadowed pre-mount directory while every host-side check reads healthy.\n      Proven by `docker compose exec review-bot sh -c 'stat -c %D /var/lib/rebar/gate-scratch;\n      stat -c %D /var/lib/rebar'` returning TWO DIFFERENT device ids, and by\n      `.gate-scratch-mounted` being visible inside the container.\n- [ ] AC7 -- the delivery gap is closed as a CLASS, and ONE mechanism is CHOSEN rather than\n      left as an either/or. **Chosen: the apply-time signal, not a post-boot reconciler.**\n      Positive rationale, not elimination: a reconciler could safely auto-MOUNT an already\n      formatted volume, but this one was RAW, and auto-`mkfs` on a volume that merely looks\n      blank is how a restored backup is destroyed -- so that branch cannot cover the case that\n      actually occurred, and a half-covering automation is worse than an honest check.\n      **Invocation point, stated:** `scripts/assert_volumes_in_service.py` is a MANDATORY\n      post-apply step, invoked from `infra/runbooks/gate-scratch-mount.md` §10 after this\n      remediation and after ANY future `terraform apply` that adds or replaces a volume.\n      Operation-linked deliberately, rather than bound to one CI provider, which is what\n      `project.portability` requires.\n      **Why it is not ALSO wired into `terraform-drift.yml`**, as a checkable fact rather than\n      an omission: that workflow assumes the `rebar-terraform-plan` OIDC role, which is\n      `ReadOnlyAccess` + `kms:Decrypt` (`infra/terraform/iam.tf:178-191`). `ssm:SendCommand` is\n      a WRITE action absent from `ReadOnlyAccess`, so that role cannot run the host probe at\n      all. Granting it is an IAM widening with its own blast radius and needs its own ticket.\n      A fix that only mounts this one volume by hand would leave the next to be found the same\n      way -- by an alarm, days later."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788651007464574001,"uuid":"3e5fd17f-3b6b-40c6-a457-cee79b2c4040"}